#section("V. Conclusion")

The promise that whoever believes "should not perish, but have eternal life"
@bible[John 3:16] can be read in two registers, and the whole distance between
Kant and eternal progression is the distance between them. Read as the gift of an
_unending life_ in which the moral vocation is pursued without term, it is Kant's:
eternal life as the endless field across which the will approaches a holiness it
never seizes, held as a hope reason may posit but not verify. Read as the promise
of _becoming like_ the one who gives it @bible[1 John 3:2], it is the tradition's:
eternal life as the medium of a real transformation whose terminus is participation
in the divine nature itself.

Both readings honor the same refusal — that salvation is not a single static
moment but a life-long, indeed eternal, ascent. Neither collapses into the other.
The philosopher's asymptote and the prophet's arrival are not two names for one
doctrine; they are two answers to a shared question, given under two warrants, and
resolving into two Gods. To see them clearly is to see that the resemblance which
first invited the comparison — perfection as trajectory — is the beginning of the
inquiry and not its conclusion. Where the trajectory leads, and whether one may
claim to know, is where Kant and the Saints part company; and it is there, not in
the shared refusal that unites them, that each tradition says what is most its own.
